TEST 6057 -- ARP Learning with full tables
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-24 (device clock 2026-09-23 21:27-21:33 UTC), tb470
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4), router MAC 0000.cd37.0d6f
Case:   num_steps = 0 in ck.db. Tested as the title states: fill the DUT's ARP table
        through its OWN resolution, then see how learning behaves when it is full.
        (Replaces the 2026-09-22 PARTIAL, which could not fill the table: the DUT does not
        learn from gratuitous ARP -- 6000 sent, 0 learned -- and there were no responders.)

VERDICT: PASS for ARP learning up to capacity; the full-table behaviour is MEASURED and
         is a finding for review, not a clean pass:
         the table caps at 2045 entries, and while it is full the DUT CANNOT RESOLVE A NEW
         REAL NEIGHBOUR -- `ping` fails with "sendmsg: No buffer space available".

METHOD
  - DUT: temporary `ip address 10.90.0.1/16 secondary` on vlan1 (on-link targets).
  - tb470 eth1 sends routed UDP (src 10.38.215.1, dst 10.90.x.y, one frame per address,
    300 pps) to the DUT router MAC, so the DUT must ARP for every destination.
  - An ARP responder on tb470 eth3 (vlan1, stack port3.0.9) answers every who-has for
    10.90.0.0/16 with a unique MAC 02:90:00:00:hi:lo, and counts requests seen.
  - After each batch: count the 10.90 rows in `show arp`.

STEP 0 -- baseline: 10.90 rows in `show arp` = 0 (5 real neighbours).

FILL
      addresses sent   DUT ARP requests seen   10.90 rows in show arp
           500                  500                     500
          1000                 1000                    1000
          2000                 2000                    2000
          4000                 2040                    2040
          6000                 2040                    2040     <- plateau
  Final `show arp`: 2045 rows = 2040 x 10.90 (10.90.1.0 .. 10.90.8.247, learned in order)
  + 5 real neighbours. Every request the DUT sent got a reply and became an entry (1:1).
  Once the table held 2045 entries the DUT sent NO further ARP requests at all.

WITH THE TABLE FULL
      ping 10.38.215.65 (already in the table)   3/3
      ping 10.10.10.2   (already in the table)   3/3
      ping 10.38.215.2  (x230, NOT in the table)  0/3
          ping: sendmsg: No buffer space available   (x3)
  Re-checked at 21:32:42, about 2 min after the last fill: 2040 x 10.90 rows still
  present, and 10.38.215.2 still 0/3 with the same error. `show log` recorded nothing
  about ARP or table exhaustion. The stack stayed `Normal operation` throughout.

RECOVERY
  `no ip address 10.90.0.1/16 secondary` -> `show arp` dropped to 1 row (10.10.10.2 on
  vlan10): the removal flushed the WHOLE vlan1 neighbour table, the real hosts included.
      ping 10.38.215.2 -> 3/3, and it is learned: 10.38.215.2 001a.eb91.cca1 vlan1 sa2
  Host paths afterwards: eth1->.10, eth1->.2, eth2->.40, eth2->.41, eth3->.66,
  eth1->10.10.10.2 all 0% loss.

FOR REVIEW
  1. Capacity ~2045 ARP entries on this build. Not documented here; could be deliberate.
  2. At capacity new neighbours are refused (ENOBUFS), not admitted by evicting stale
     entries, and nothing is logged. Entries learned 2+ min earlier and never used again
     were still held. Whether this is the intended full-table policy is the question the
     case exists to ask -- a DUT whose table is filled by transient destinations stops
     reaching new real neighbours until the entries age out or are cleared.
  3. Not measured: how long until entries age out on their own (the default ARP timeout
     was not waited out).

Cleanup: secondary removed (vlan1 = .10/.40/.66 as before), responder stopped.
